Rule: the first 3 lessons of a paid course are a free preview.

// preview.ts
export function canPreview(orderIndex: number): boolean {
  return orderIndex <= 3;
}

// preview.test.ts
it.each([
  [1, true],
  [10, false],
])("canPreview(%i) is %s", (orderIndex, expected) => {
  expect(canPreview(orderIndex)).toBe(expected);
});

$ npx vitest run preview
 ✓ preview.test.ts (2 tests) 3ms

Statement: "If someone changes <= 3 to < 3, at least one of these two tests fails."

Course "TypeScript Basics" has 4 required lessons. A learner had finished lessons 1 and 2 (progress 50%) and double-clicked "Mark as complete" on lesson 3. API access log:

14:02:07.311 POST /lessons/3/complete 200 41ms
14:02:07.402 POST /lessons/3/complete 200 38ms
14:02:07.530 GET  /courses/ts-basics/progress 200 {"percent":100}

The progress code:

function progressPercent(requiredIds: number[], completions: { lessonId: number }[]) {
  const done = completions.filter((c) => requiredIds.includes(c.lessonId)).length;
  return Math.floor((done * 100) / requiredIds.length);
}

Statement: "A unit test that calls progressPercent([1, 2, 3, 4], [{ lessonId: 1 }, { lessonId: 2 }, { lessonId: 3 }, { lessonId: 3 }]) and expects 75 fails on this code."
